  @minus{}@U{03C0}
@item * @tab @var{y} = +0.0 @tab @var{x} = 0 @tab
  @U{03C0}/2
@item * @tab @var{y} = @minus{}0.0 @tab @var{x} = 0 @tab
  @minus{}@U{03C0}/2
@end multitable
@end indentedblock
@end ifhtml

The above specification follows @cite{Common @sc{LISP}: The Language,
second edition}@footnote{@value{refCommonLispTheLanguage}}, which in
turn cites @i{Principal values and branch cuts in complex
APL}@footnote{@value{refPrincipalValues}}; refer to these sources for
more detailed discussion of branch cuts, boundary conditions, and
implementation of these functions.  When it is possible, these
procedures produce a real result from a real argument.
@end deffn

@deffn procedure square z

Returns the square of @var{z}.  This is equivalent to @nc{(* @rv{z}
@rv{z})}.

@lisp
(square 42)  @ok{1764}
(square 2.0) @ok{4.0}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn {inexact library procedure} sqrt z

Returns the principal square root of @var{z}.  The result will have
either a positive real part, or a zero real part and a non-negative
imaginary part.

@lisp
(sqrt 9)  @ok{3}
(sqrt -1) @ok{+i}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure exact-integer-sqrt k

Returns two non-negative exact integers @var{s} and @var{r} where
@var{k} = @square{@var{s}} + @var{r} and @var{k} @U{003C}
@square{(@var{s} + 1)}

@lisp
(exact-integer-sqrt 4) @ok{2 0}
(exact-integer-sqrt 5) @ok{2 1}
@end lisp
@end deffn

@deffn procedure expt @parI{z} @parII{z}

Returns @varI{z} raised to the power @varII{z}.  For nonzero @varI{z},
this is

@display
@varI{z}@sup{@varII{z}} = @i{e}@sup{@varII{z}log(@varI{z})}
@end display

The value of 0@sup{@var{z}} is 1 if @nc{(zero? @rv{z})}, 0 if
@nc{(real-part @rv{z})} is positive, and an error otherwise.  Similarly
for 0.0@sup{@var{z}}, with inexact results.
@end deffn

@deffn {complex library procedure} make-rectangular @parI{x} @parII{x}
@deffnx {complex library procedure} make-polar @parIII{x} @parIV{x}
@deffnx {complex library procedure} real-part z
@deffnx {complex library procedure} imag-part z
@deffnx {complex library procedure} magnitude z
@deffnx {complex library procedure} angle z

Let @varI{x}, @varII{x}, @varIII{x}, and @varIV{x} be real numbers and
@var{z} be a complex number such that

@display
@var{z} = @varI{x} + @varII{x}@i{i} = @varIII{x} @U{2219} @i{e}@sup{@i{i}@varIV{x}}
@end display

Then all of

@ifnothtml
@indentedblock
@multitable {(make-rectangular x1 x2)} {} {x__angle_}
@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}make-rectangular @r{@varI{x}} @r{@varII{x}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @var{z}

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}make-polar @r{@varI{x}} @r{@varII{x}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @var{z}

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}real-part @r{@var{z}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @varI{x}

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}imag-part @r{@var{z}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @varII{x}

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}magnitude @r{@var{z}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab |@varIII{x}|

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}angle @r{@var{z}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @var{x@sub{angle}}
@end multitable
@end indentedblock
@end ifnothtml
@ifhtml
@indentedblock
@multitable @columnfractions .40 .04 .56
@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}make-rectangular @r{@varI{x}} @r{@varII{x}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @var{z}

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}make-polar @r{@varI{x}} @r{@varII{x}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @var{z}

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}real-part @r{@var{z}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @varI{x}

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}imag-part @r{@var{z}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @varII{x}

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}magnitude @r{@var{z}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab |@varIII{x}|

@item @t{@compound{@U{0028}}angle @r{@var{z}}@compound{@U{0029}}}
@tab @t{@result{}} @tab @var{x@sub{angle}}
@end multitable
@end indentedblock
@end ifhtml

are true, where @minus{}@U{03C0} @leq{} @var{x@sub{angle}} @leq{}
@U{03C0} with @var{x@sub{angle}} = @varIV{x} + 2@U{03C0}@var{n} for some
integer @var{n}.

The @nc{make-polar} procedure may return an inexact complex number even
if its arguments are exact.  The @nc{real-part} and @nc{imag-part}
procedures may return exact real numbers when applied to an inexact
complex number if the corresponding argument passed to
@nc{make-rectangular} was exact.

The @code{magnitude} procedure is the same as @code{abs} for a real
argument, but @code{abs} is in the base library, whereas
@code{magnitude} is in the optional complex library.
@end deffn

@deffn procedure inexact z
@deffnx procedure exact z
The procedure @code{inexact} returns an inexact representation of
@var{z}.  The value returned is the inexact number that is numerically
closest to the argument.  For inexact arguments, the result is the same
as the argument.  For exact complex numbers, the result is a complex
number whose real and imaginary parts are the result of applying
@code{inexact} to the real and imaginary parts of the argument,
respectively.  If an exact argument has no reasonably close inexact
equivalent (in the sense of @code{=}), then a violation of an
implementation restriction may be reported.

The procedure @code{exact} returns an exact representation of @var{z}.
The value returned is the exact number that is numerically closest to
the argument.  For exact arguments, the result is the same as the
argument.  For inexact non-integral real arguments, the implementation
may return a rational approximation, or may report an implementation
violation.  For inexact complex arguments, the result is a complex
number whose real and imaginary parts are the result of applying
@code{exact} to the real and imaginary parts of the argument,
respectively.  If an inexact argument has no reasonably close exact
equivalent, (in the sense of @code{=}), then a violation of an
implementation restriction may be reported.

These procedures implement the natural one-to-one correspondence between
exact and inexact integers throughout an implementation-dependent range.
@xref{Implementation restrictions}.

@quotation
@i{Note:} These procedures were known in @value{R5RS} as
@nc{exact->inexact} and @nc{inexact->exact}, respectively, but they have
always accepted arguments of any exactness.  The new names are clearer
and shorter, as well as being compatible with @value{R6RS}.
@end quotation
@end deffn

@node Numerical input and output
@subsection Numerical input and output
@deffn procedure number->string z
@deffnx procedure number->string z radix

It is an error if @var{radix} is not one of 2, 8, 10, or 16.

The procedure @nc{number->string} takes a number and a radix and returns
as a string an external representation of the given number in the given
radix such that

@example grammar
(let ((@letlikebind{number} @rv{number})
      (@letlikebind{radix} @rv{radix}))
  (eqv? number
        (string->number (number->string number
                                        radix)
                        radix)))
@end example

is true.  It is an error if no possible result makes this expression
true.  If omitted, @var{radix} defaults to 10.

If @var{z} is inexact, the radix is 10, and the above expression can be
satisfied by a result that contains a decimal point, then the result
contains a decimal point and is expressed using the minimum number of
digits (exclusive of exponent and trailing zeroes) needed to make the
above expression true@footnote{@value{refPrintFPQuicklyAccurately}}
@footnote{@value{refReadFPAccurately}}; otherwise the format of the
result is unspecified.

The result returned by @nc{number->string} never contains an explicit
radix prefix.

@quotation
@i{Note:} The error case can occur only when @var{z} is not a complex
number or is a complex number with a non-rational real or imaginary
part.
@end quotation

@quotation
@i{Rationale:} If @var{z} is an inexact number and the radix is 10, then
the above expression is normally satisfied by a result containing a
decimal point.  The unspecified case allows for infinities, NaNs, and
unusual representations.
@end quotation
@end deffn

@deffn procedure string->number string
@deffnx procedure string->number string radix

Returns a number of the maximally precise representation expressed by
the given @code{string}.  It is an error if @code{radix} is not 2, 8,
10, or 16.

If supplied, @code{radix} is a default radix that will be overridden if
an explicit radix prefix is present in string (e.g.@: @code{"#o177"}).
If @code{radix} is not supplied, then the default radix is 10.  If
@code{string} is not a syntactically valid notation for a number, or
would result in a number that the implementation cannot represent, then
@nc{string->number} returns @code{#f}.  An error is never signaled due
to the content of @var{string}.

@lisp
(string->number "100")    @ok{100}
(string->number "100" 16) @ok{256}
(string->number "1e2")    @ok{100.0}
@end lisp

@quotation
@i{Note:} The domain of @nc{string->number} may be restricted by
implementations in the following ways.  If all numbers supported by an
implementation are real, then @nc{string->number} is permitted to return
@code{#f} whenever @code{string} uses the polar or rectangular notations
for complex numbers.  If all numbers are integers, then
@nc{string->number} may return @code{#f} whenever the fractional
notation is used.  If all numbers are exact, then @nc{string->number}
may return @code{#f} whenever an exponent marker or explicit exactness
aprefix is used.  If all inexact numbers are integers, then
@nc{string->number} may return @code{#f} whenever a decimal point is
used.

The rules used by a particular implementation for @nc{string->number}
must also be applied to @code{read} and to the routine that reads
programs, in order to maintain consistency between internal numeric
processing, @nobr{@acronym{I/O,input/output}}, and the processing of
programs.  As a consequence, the @value{R5RS} permission to return
@code{#f} when @var{string} has an explicit radix prefix has been
withdrawn.
@end quotation
@end deffn
